      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The area was historically inhabited by the Caddo and Atakapas Indians before European settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/big-thicket",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Oil and lumber booms in the Big Thicket spurred conservation that culminated in the 2024 50th anniversary of the National Preserve.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.nps.gov/bith/learn/historyculture/index.htm",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Big Thicket National Preserve was established in twelve separate units across Polk and Tyler counties, preserving a unique mix of pine, oak, and swamp habitats.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/big-thicket",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The name “Big Thicket” originally described a heavily wooded area south of the Old San Antonio Road, east of the Brazos, north of the coastal prairie, and west of the Sabine.",
